# Design changes (Groundwork)

## Updating a design that already exists

Change the real pages under `packages/server/public/` (the groundworklearn.com site, and the CSS/JS it uses). Run the local website server (`npm run server`, default `http://127.0.0.1:8787`) and verify in the browser. Do not make a standalone HTML prototype for a restyle, a style sweep, or a layout change to a page that already ships.

If local preview is wrong, it is not merged. Do not stop and wait for a prototype approval before editing `packages/server/public/`.

## Write the convention into project docs

When a page update settles a design choice another page could follow, document that convention in the repo (for example `docs/design/design-language.md` when that file exists, or a short note in the PR). A choice is a convention when a second page would otherwise invent it: spacing, a type role, how a control moves, what stays on screen, empty and error. A one-off wording change is not a convention.

## A brand-new feature that needs a new design

When the feature has no existing page to put the design on, a lightweight HTML prototype comes first, then implementation. Path: `docs/design/prototypes/<issue-key>-<slug>.html` (use your Jira/GitHub issue key, e.g. `GW-42-profile-cards.html`). Never under `packages/server/public/` and never deployed as production Hosting except a temporary preview link.

### How prototypes are built

- Link the real stylesheets by **relative path** from the prototype file, e.g. `../../../packages/server/public/styles.css`.
- Match typography to the live site: copy the font `<link>` pattern from `packages/server/public/index.html` (Jost) or link that Google Fonts URL explicitly.
- Use the same class names and tokens as the live site where possible; prototype-only CSS goes in an inline `<style>` block.
- **Fictional data only** — no real learner emails, vault paths, or account content.
- Must read correctly at **390px** and **1280px** width.
- Show **empty**, **one**, **many**, and **error** states on the same page (labelled sections).
- When there is a real design choice, show **2–3 structurally different variants** labelled **A / B / C** (not colour-only tweaks).

### Review passes (before the draft prototype PR)

1. **`frontend-design`** — layout, hierarchy, copy, critique pass (Groundwork override: match the existing site voice and tokens).
2. **`web-interface-guidelines`** — run against the prototype file per `.cursor/skills/web-interface-guidelines/SKILL.md`.

### Draft prototype PR

1. Commit **only** the prototype (and any docs tied to it).
2. Open a **draft** PR titled `[Prototype] <issue-key>` (not the implementation PR).
3. Attach screenshots at **390×844** and **1280×800**.
4. Post a view link (primary format below) with a **3-line summary** of what changed and what you want feedback on.
5. Open the link yourself and confirm site CSS loaded (fonts, layout, token-driven styles).
6. **Stop** until Jonathan approves. Implementation follows in a separate branch/PR.

### View links

Use the commit SHA that contains the prototype file.

- **Primary:** `https://htmlpreview.github.io/?https://github.com/JonathanHarris15/Groundwork/blob/<commitSHA>/docs/design/prototypes/<file>.html`
- **Backup:** `https://raw.githack.com/JonathanHarris15/Groundwork/<commitSHA>/docs/design/prototypes/<file>.html`

## When a prototype is the wrong tool

- The page already exists and the task is to restyle it, clean it up, or align it with existing site patterns
- Pure bug fix with no layout change
- Copy-only change
- Style fix of **10 lines or fewer** with no layout change
- Obsidian-plugin-only UI with no website counterpart (use plugin manual testing instead)

## Smoke test

Prototype-only commits **skip** the jev smoke test. Any PR that changes `packages/server/public/` or other user-visible UI still runs jev in full per `.cursor/rules/smoke-test.mdc`.

## Supersedes in-app `?variant=` prototypes

This rule supersedes the `?variant=` on-route approach in `.cursor/skills/prototype/UI.md` and related prototype docs **unless Jonathan asks for in-app variants**. A restyle still lands on the real site. A new feature's prototype lives in `docs/design/prototypes/`.
